    "created_at": "2026-10-27T11:33:55Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-27T11:33:55Z",
    "body": "To make this fail-safe, `forget` called after `open` must win. If `forget` can reject, an application bug could unexpectedly leave a permission granted after the site asked to revoke it. Permission revocations can come from other sources, like browser permissions UI or changes to policy-granted permissions; these cases should never fail to revoke the permission and should always sever the device connection even if there are concurrent device operations. I think `forget` should work similarly.\n\n`open` should re-check permissions after opening the device. Currently, step 4.4 queues a task that always resolves the promise with undefined. Instead, it should check if the permission is still granted and reject with `NotAllowedError` if the permission was lost.\n\nThe spec doesn't describe how permissions are stored or checked, we should improve this in addition to adding the permission check in the `open` step. The current language is too vague: \"If the user has allowed the site to access device as the result of a previous call to requestDevice()\" doesn't consider whether access may have been revoked after the call to `requestDevice`.\n\nWebBluetooth stores granted permissions as [`AllowedBluetoothDevice`](https://bluetooth.spec.whatwg.org/#dictdef-allowedbluetoothdevice) objects in a [`BluetoothPermissionStorage`](https://bluetooth.spec.whatwg.org/#dictdef-bluetoothpermissionstorage); similarly, WebUSB stores granted permissions as [`AllowedUSBDevice`](https://usb.spec.whatwg.org/#dictdef-allowedusbdevice) objects in a [`USBPermissionStorage`](https://usb.spec.whatwg.org/#dictdef-usbpermissionstorage). We should add `AllowedHIDDevice`/`HIDPermissionStorage` for WebHID.",
    "author_association": "COLLABORATOR",
    "pin": null,
